{"name":"Olm Pink","hex":"#ffe6e2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"olm","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Oloma."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Proteus anguinus, a cave-dwelling neotenous salamander with external gills, found along the coast from northeastern Italy to Montenegro."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/olm"},{"term":"pink","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A color reminiscent of pinks, the flowers.","Magenta, the colour evoked by red and blue light when combined."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a colour between red and white; pale red.","Of a fox-hunter's jacket: scarlet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pink"}],"entries":[{"title":"Olm","blocks":[{"text":"Olms are the nine species in the genus Proteus. They are aquatic salamanders, and the only exclusively cave-dwelling chordate genus found in Europe. The genus is assigned to the family Proteidae, along with Necturus. In contrast to most amphibians, this genus is entirely aquatic; eating, sleeping, and breeding underwater. Living in caves found in the Dinaric Alps, the genus is endemic to the waters that flow underground through the extensive limestone bedrock of the karst of Central and Southeastern Europe in the basin of the Soča River near Trieste, Italy, southern Slovenia, southwestern Croatia, and Bosnia and Herzegovina. Introduced populations are found near Vicenza, Italy, and Kranj, Slovenia. It was first mentioned in 1689 by the local naturalist Valvasor in his Glory of the Duchy of Carniola, who reported that, after heavy rains, the olms were washed up from the underground waters and were believed by local people to be a cave dragon's offspring."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Proteus_anguinus_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olm","wikidata":"Q15856"},{"title":"Pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink is a pale tint of red or rose, the color of the pink flower. It was first used as a color name in the late 17th century. A combination of pink and white is associated with innocence, whereas a combination of pink and black links to eroticism and seduction."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg/330px-Pink_sapphire_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink","wikidata":"Q429220"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Proteus_anguinus_Postojnska_Jama_Slovenija.jpg/500px-Proteus_anguinus_Postojnska_Jama_Slovenija.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Proteus_anguinus_Postojnska_Jama_Slovenija.jpg/500px-Proteus_anguinus_Postojnska_Jama_Slovenija.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AProteus_anguinus_Postojnska_Jama_Slovenija.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Prot%C3%A9e_aveugle.jpg/500px-Prot%C3%A9e_aveugle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Prot%C3%A9e_aveugle.jpg/1280px-Prot%C3%A9e_aveugle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Prot%C3%A9e_aveugle.jpg/500px-Prot%C3%A9e_aveugle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The olm as depicted by the French biologist Gaston Bonnier in 1907","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AProt%C3%A9e_aveugle.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/P_anguinus-head1.jpg/250px-P_anguinus-head1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/P_anguinus-head1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/P_anguinus-head1.jpg/250px-P_anguinus-head1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The front part of the olm's head carries sensitive chemo-, mechano-, and electroreceptors.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AP_anguinus-head1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/P_anguinus1.jpg/500px-P_anguinus1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/P_anguinus1.jpg/1280px-P_anguinus1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/P_anguinus1.jpg/500px-P_anguinus1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The olm swims by serpentine bending of the body.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AP_anguinus1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/P_anguinus-head.jpg/250px-P_anguinus-head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/P_anguinus-head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/P_anguinus-head.jpg/250px-P_anguinus-head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Disproportionately elongated head with gills","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AP_anguinus-head.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/P_anguinus_parkelj-head.jpg/250px-P_anguinus_parkelj-head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2e/P_anguinus_parkelj-head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/P_anguinus_parkelj-head.jpg/250px-P_anguinus_parkelj-head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The black olm, a subspecies, has a shorter head with more-developed eyes compared to the nominate subspecies.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AP_anguinus_parkelj-head.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Researchventhole.jpg/500px-Researchventhole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Researchventhole.jpg/1280px-Researchventhole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Researchventhole.jpg/500px-Researchventhole.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The research vent-hole in Jelševnik near Črnomelj, where quality checks of water and sediments are performed regularly, and where activities of black olms are registered with an IR camera","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AResearchventhole.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Proteus_-_Laurenti.jpg/500px-Proteus_-_Laurenti.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Proteus_-_Laurenti.jpg/500px-Proteus_-_Laurenti.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sketch of the olm in Specimen Medicum, Exhibens Synopsin Reptilium Emendatam cum Experimentis circa Venena (1768) by Josephus Nicolaus Laurenti","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik